Post: Database Design Best Practices Every Backend Dev Should Know
URL: https://roobystudio.com/posts/db-design-best-practices/

==============================
LinkedIn (long)
==============================

A bad API can be versioned. A bad database schema haunts you forever.

I wrote about the database habits I keep coming back to — not textbook trivia, but the tradeoffs that show up in real backends: when a physical `FOREIGN KEY` is worth it vs when a logical FK is the pragmatic choice, how N+1 problems hide in the ORM layer, and how to pick between optimistic and pessimistic locking so you get fewer subtle bugs and deadlocks.

There’s also a companion thread on normalization and when to denormalize on purpose, plus the “small stuff” that compounds: indexing what you actually query, soft deletes, audit columns, and naming conventions that make the next person’s life easier (often future you, on call at 2am).

Pair read with the earlier piece on 1NF–3NF if you want the full arc: shape first, then constraints and operations.

Read it here: https://roobystudio.com/posts/db-design-best-practices/

#backend #database #sql #softwareengineering

