==============================
X / Twitter — thread (optional)
==============================

1/6 A bad API can be versioned. A bad database schema haunts you forever. I wrote up the DB design practices I keep revisiting — not theory for its own sake, but tradeoffs that bite in production.

https://roobystudio.com/posts/db-design-best-practices/

2/6 Normalization first (pair with the 1NF–3NF post), then deliberate denormalization when reads need it — not by accident.

3/6 Physical FK = DB-enforced integrity. Logical FK = same column, app/docs own the rule — common across services, big migrations, or out-of-order loads. Know which gatekeeper you’re choosing.

4/6 N+1 isn’t “SQL is slow” — it’s often the ORM loading graphs row by row. Fix at the mapping/query layer.

5/6 Concurrency: optimistic vs pessimistic locking — wrong choice shows up as subtle data bugs or deadlocks, not just slow queries.

6/6 Indexes for what you query, soft deletes when you need recovery/audit, timestamps (and actors where it matters), consistent naming. Small habits; big payoff.

